Interview-style questions on REST status codes & ResponseEntity

Conceptual / HTTP semantics
What is the difference between 401 Unauthorized and 403 Forbidden?
What's the difference between idempotent and safe HTTP methods? Which methods fall into each category?
Why is POST neither safe nor idempotent, while GET, PUT, and DELETE are idempotent (and GET is also safe)?
What status code would you return for a successful resource creation, and why is 200 generally considered incorrect for that case?
What is the purpose of the Location header, and in which response types (status codes) is it used?
What's the difference between 204 No Content and 200 OK with an empty body? Why does the distinction matter to API consumers?
Explain the difference between PUT and PATCH. When would you use one over the other?
If a PUT request is sent to a URI that doesn't exist yet, what should the server do — create it (201) or return 404? Justify your answer.
What does idempotency mean in the context of PUT, and why does this make it suitable for safe retries by clients (e.g., after a network timeout)?

Spring / ResponseEntity specific
What is ResponseEntity in Spring, and why would you use it instead of just returning the object directly from a @RestController method?
What's the difference between ResponseEntity.ok(body) and ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.OK).body(body)?
How do you set a custom header (e.g., Location, ETag) on a ResponseEntity? Walk through the builder API.
What's the difference between ResponseEntity.ok() and ResponseEntity.ok(body) in terms of return type and chaining capability?
How would you use ResponseEntity.created(URI location) in a controller, and what does it set automatically?
How do you build a resource URI dynamically in a Spring controller after creating it (e.g., using ServletUriComponentsBuilder or UriComponentsBuilder)?
What issue can occur with ServletUriComponentsBuilder.fromCurrentRequest() when your app runs behind a reverse proxy/load balancer, and how do you fix it?

Error handling / API design
How do you handle a "resource not found" scenario in a Spring REST API — manually in the controller, or via a global exception handler? Which is considered best practice and why?
What is @ControllerAdvice / @RestControllerAdvice, and how does it relate to returning proper HTTP status codes for exceptions?
Why is it considered bad practice to return 200 OK for all responses (including errors) with an error flag in the body instead of using proper HTTP status codes?
Design a REST API response contract: for a POST /resource that creates something, a GET /resource/{id} that fetches it, a PUT /resource/{id} that replaces it, and a DELETE /resource/{id} — specify the exact status code you'd return for each in the success case.

Scenario-based (common in practical interviews)
You're building a bulk-import endpoint where some records succeed and others fail validation. What status code would you return for a partial success, and how would you structure the response body?
A client complains that retrying a failed POST /orders call due to a network timeout created duplicate orders. How would you redesign the API to prevent this (hint: idempotency keys)?
Your API currently returns 200 for a DELETE operation with a JSON body { "message": "deleted successfully" }. A reviewer flags this in code review — what would you change and why?
Want me to pick a handful of these and turn them into a mock interview Q&A session (you answer, I evaluate)?

Interview questions — @Embeddable / @Embedded (JPA)
Conceptual
What is the difference between an @Entity and an @Embeddable in JPA?
Why doesn't an @Embeddable class require an @Id field?
Does an @Embeddable object get its own database table? Explain what actually happens at the schema level when you use @Embedded.
Can an @Embeddable class be persisted independently, without being part of an owning entity? Why or why not?
Why does @Embeddable require a no-arg constructor?
What's the lifecycle relationship between an embeddable object and its owning entity? (Hint: think cascade/orphan removal vs. embedded fields.)

Column mapping / schema
If two entities both have an @Embedded Money amount field, do they share the same table or get separate columns in their own tables?
If you need two different @Embedded Money fields in the same entity (e.g., originalAmount and refundedAmount), what problem would you run into, and how do you solve it? (Hint: @AttributeOverrides.)
How does Hibernate decide the column names for the fields inside an @Embeddable class (e.g., amountUnits → amount_units)?
Can an @Embeddable contain another @Embeddable (nested embedding)? Is that supported in JPA?

Querying
How would you write a Spring Data JPA derived query method to find all Refund entities with currency = "INR", given currency lives inside the embedded Money object?
How would you write the equivalent JPQL query referencing an embedded field?
Can you create an index on a column that comes from an @Embeddable field? How?

Design / comparison
When would you choose @Embeddable/@Embedded over a @ManyToOne/@OneToOne relationship to a separate entity? Give a concrete example of each scenario.
What are the downsides or limitations of using @Embeddable instead of a full entity (e.g., can't query it independently, no identity, no separate lifecycle)?
Why is Money a good candidate for @Embeddable rather than making it a full @Entity with its own table and foreign key?
In DDD (Domain-Driven Design) terms, what concept does @Embeddable typically correspond to? (Hint: Value Object vs. Entity.)

Behavioral / validation
In the given Money class, add() and subtract() throw IllegalArgumentException if currencies don't match. Is this validation enforced by JPA/Hibernate, or is it purely application-level logic? What does that imply about where business rules for embeddables should live?
Since Money is mutable (has @Setter... actually note: it only has @Getter, no setter) — is Money effectively immutable here? Why might immutability matter for a value object representing money?
What could go wrong if Money did NOT override equals()/hashCode() properly (notice @EqualsAndHashCode is present) — especially if used as part of a @Embeddable compared across entities or stored in collections?

Scenario-based
You need to store a Money value that can be null (e.g., an optional field). What JPA annotation/configuration issue might you run into with embeddables and null values, and how would you handle it?
If you wanted to add validation like @NotNull or @Positive on Money.amountUnits, where would you place those annotations — on the Money class itself, or on the field in the owning entity? Does Bean Validation work with embeddables?
How would schema migration (e.g., Flyway/Liquibase) differ if Money were an @Embeddable (inline columns) versus a separate @Entity with a foreign key, in terms of migration complexity when adding a new currency-related field?
